  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What turns the minute hand?",
      "a": "The minute hand is fixed to a shaft that the clock's gear train turns exactly once an hour, at a speed controlled by the escapement.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the minute hand move?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why use two pairs of gears?",
      "a": "A 12 to 1 slow-down in one step would need a huge gear and a tiny one. Two steps, such as 3 to 1 and 4 to 1, multiply to 12 to 1 with gears that fit behind the face.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a compound gear train?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do both hands turn from the same centre?",
      "a": "The hour wheel is mounted on a hollow tube that fits over the minute hand's shaft. The minute hand fits on the shaft and the hour hand on the tube, so they share a centre but turn at different speeds.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are the clock hands on one spindle?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the hour hand move 12 times slower than the minute hand?",
      "a": "The minute hand goes round once an hour, and the hour hand must go round once in 12 hours. So the gears between them slow the motion by 12 times.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the gear ratio between clock hands?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What turns the second hand?",
      "a": "In many pendulum clocks the escape wheel turns once a minute, so the second hand is fixed to its shaft. Another set of gears links it to the minute shaft with a 60 to 1 slow-down.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the second hand work?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In a two-step gear train, the overall ratio is the two ratios multiplied together.",
    "The minute hand turns 12 times faster than the hour hand and 60 times slower than a second hand."
  ],
